export const M6={title:'Infection control, asepsis and sterile gloving (Skill VIII)',hook:'Medical offices see sick and healthy patients side by side and perform procedures from injections to minor surgery. Breaking the chain of infection protects everyone, and Skill VIII—sterile gloving in three minutes—tests the core of surgical asepsis.',objectives:['Apply the chain of infection, standard precautions and transmission-based precautions.','Distinguish medical asepsis from surgical asepsis.','Compare sanitization, disinfection and sterilization, including autoclave use and monitoring.','Apply sterile gloves and maintain a sterile field following Skill VIII.'],sections:[
['The chain of infection and precautions',[
'Infection requires an **infectious agent**, a **reservoir**, a **portal of exit**, a **means of transmission**, a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**; breaking any link prevents disease. **Standard precautions** apply to every patient: **hand hygiene**, PPE when contact with blood or body fluids is expected, respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette, safe injection practices, sharps safety and cleaning and disinfecting equipment and surfaces. **Transmission-based precautions** are added for known or suspected infections: **contact** (gloves and gown—for example, MRSA or *C. difficile*), **droplet** (a mask within close range—influenza, pertussis) and **airborne** (an N95 respirator and an airborne infection isolation room—tuberculosis, measles, chickenpox). The **OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard** requires an exposure control plan, free hepatitis B vaccination for at-risk employees, PPE, engineering controls such as safety needles and sharps containers, training and post-exposure follow-up.',
sort('Which precaution is added?',['Contact','Droplet','Airborne'],[['MRSA wound infection',0],['C. difficile diarrhea',0],['Influenza',1],['Pertussis (whooping cough)',1],['Tuberculosis',2],['Measles',2]])]],
['Medical and surgical asepsis',[
'**Medical asepsis** (clean technique) reduces the number and spread of microorganisms—hand hygiene, clean gloves, cleaning surfaces and proper waste handling. **Surgical asepsis** (sterile technique) eliminates all microorganisms from an area or object and is used for procedures that enter sterile body tissue: minor surgery, sterile dressing changes and catheterization. Rules of a **sterile field**: only sterile items touch sterile items; a **1-inch border** around a sterile field is considered contaminated; keep sterile items **above waist level** and in view; never turn your back on a sterile field or reach across it; do not talk, cough or sneeze over it; if in doubt, consider an item contaminated; and wet areas allow microorganisms to wick through (strike-through), contaminating the field.',
sort('Medical or surgical asepsis?',['Medical asepsis','Surgical asepsis'],[['Handwashing before rooming a patient',0],['Wearing sterile gloves for minor surgery',1],['Disinfecting the exam table',0],['Setting up a sterile tray',1],['Clean gloves for a urine test',0],['Keeping hands above the waist with sterile gloves',1]])]],
['Sanitization, disinfection and sterilization',[
'**Sanitization** removes visible debris and reduces microorganisms by scrubbing instruments with detergent—always the first step. **Disinfection** destroys most pathogens but not all spores: **high-level** disinfection for semicritical items that contact mucous membranes, and **intermediate or low-level** disinfection for surfaces and noncritical equipment. **Sterilization** destroys all microorganisms, including spores. The **autoclave** sterilizes with **steam under pressure**, commonly at about **250°F (121°C)** and **15 psi** for about **20–30 minutes** for wrapped items (times vary by load and manufacturer). Instruments are sanitized, dried, opened (hinged instruments in the open position) and wrapped or pouched with indicators; loads are not overcrowded. **Chemical indicators** (tape or strips that change color) show that conditions were reached, and **biological indicators** with heat-resistant spores, tested at least weekly, confirm sterilization. Packs are labeled with the date, contents and initials and stored in clean, dry areas; packs that are torn or wet are considered contaminated.',
order('Instrument processing',['Wear PPE and rinse or soak instruments after use','Sanitize by scrubbing with detergent','Rinse and dry','Wrap or pouch with an indicator, hinges open','Autoclave following the manufacturer’s cycle','Dry, label with date and contents, and store'])]],
['Sterile gloving (Skill VIII)',[
'In Skill VIII (3 minutes), the competitor **removes rings and watch** and verbalizes washing hands for surgical asepsis. They **open the sterile glove package** and place it on a clean counter with the **cuff end toward the body**. Using the **non-dominant hand**, they **grasp the glove for the dominant hand by the fold of the cuff** and insert the dominant hand, pulling the glove on while **keeping the cuff turned back**. With the gloved fingers placed **under the cuff** of the other glove, they insert the non-dominant hand and ease the glove on by pulling on the **inside fold of the cuff**, **avoiding touching the thumb of the dominant hand** to the outside cuff where it was contaminated. They **smooth the gloves** over wrists and fingers, inspect for tears or holes, **keep hands above waist level** and **touch nothing except items in the sterile field**. To remove, they pull the glove off the dominant hand by grasping the palm with the thumb and fingers and turn it inside out without touching the contaminated side, slip the ungloved hand inside the top cuff of the remaining glove and peel it off inside out, dispose of the gloves and perform hand hygiene (32 points; 70% is 22.4).',
order('Sterile gloving (HOSA Skill VIII)',['Remove rings and watch; wash hands for surgical asepsis','Open the package on a clean surface, cuffs toward you','Grasp the dominant-hand glove by the cuff fold','Insert the dominant hand, keeping the cuff turned back','Slide gloved fingers under the other glove’s cuff','Insert the non-dominant hand and ease the glove on','Smooth and inspect the gloves; keep hands above the waist','Touch only sterile items; remove inside out and perform hand hygiene'])]],
['Protecting yourself and patients',[
kase('A contamination question',{a:{text:'While putting on the second sterile glove, the thumb of your gloved dominant hand brushes the inside of the cuff that your bare hand touched. What should you do?',choices:[['Consider the glove contaminated and start over with a new pair','b','Correct. If in doubt, consider it contaminated.',true],['Continue because it was only a brief touch','b','Any contact with a non-sterile surface contaminates the glove.',false]]},b:{text:'After a procedure, you are stuck by a used needle. What is the first action?',choices:[['Wash the area with soap and water, then report the exposure immediately for evaluation','c','Correct. Prompt washing, reporting and medical follow-up are required.',true],['Finish your work and report it later','c','Delays can reduce the effectiveness of post-exposure treatment.',false]]},c:{text:'You complete an exposure report and receive post-exposure evaluation according to the office’s exposure control plan.',end:true}})]],
["Assisting with minor office surgery",["Minor procedures—removing moles and cysts, repairing lacerations, draining abscesses and taking biopsies—require **surgical asepsis**. The medical assistant confirms that **informed consent** is signed, prepares the room and positions the patient, and sets up a **sterile field** on a Mayo stand. To open a sterile pack, place it with the outer flap pointing away, open the **flap farthest from you first**, then the sides and finally the flap nearest you, touching only the outside. Add sterile items by dropping them onto the field from about 6 inches without crossing over it. To pour a sterile solution, check the label three times, **palm the label** so drips do not obscure it, pour a small amount into a waste container first (lipping) and pour from a few inches above the sterile container without splashing.","Common instruments include the **scalpel** (cutting), **hemostats** (clamping vessels), **tissue and dressing forceps** (grasping), **scissors**, **needle holders** (holding suture needles) and **retractors**. **Sutures** are sized by number—**larger numbers indicate thinner suture** (6-0 is finer than 3-0)—and are absorbable or nonabsorbable. Specimens are placed in labeled containers with the correct preservative, usually formalin, and sent to pathology with a requisition. Afterward, the medical assistant applies a dressing, gives written wound-care instructions, disposes of sharps and cleans and processes instruments.",{"t":"check","q":"Which suture is finest (thinnest)?","options":["6-0","3-0","0","1"],"why":"Larger numbers before the zero mean thinner suture."}]]
],takeaways:['Standard precautions for all patients; add contact (gloves, gown), droplet (mask) or airborne (N95) precautions; OSHA requires an exposure control plan and free hepatitis B vaccine.','Medical asepsis reduces microbes; surgical asepsis eliminates them—1-inch border, above the waist, never turn your back on the field.','Sanitize → disinfect → sterilize; autoclave about 250°F at 15 psi for 20–30 minutes; Skill VIII: cuffs toward you, grasp by the fold, gloved fingers under the second cuff, hands above the waist.'],terms:[['Surgical asepsis','Technique that eliminates all microorganisms from an area.'],['Autoclave','Device that sterilizes with steam under pressure.'],['Biological indicator','Spore test that confirms sterilization.']],mcqs:[q('Which precaution is used for tuberculosis?','Airborne precautions with an N95 respirator','Contact precautions only','No precautions','Droplet precautions only','TB spreads through tiny airborne particles.'),q('Which is the first step in processing a used instrument?','Sanitization','Sterilization','Storage','Labeling','Debris must be removed first.'),q('A typical autoclave cycle uses about:','250°F (121°C) at 15 psi','100°F at 1 psi','500°F with no pressure','32°F','Steam under pressure.'),q('How wide is the contaminated border of a sterile field?','1 inch','6 inches','No border','12 inches','Treat the outer inch as non-sterile.'),q('In Skill VIII, the first glove is grasped:','By the fold of the cuff with the non-dominant hand','By the fingers','With the gloved hand','By the palm','Touch only the inside of the cuff.'),q('With sterile gloves on, hands must be kept:','Above waist level','Below the waist','Behind the back','In pockets','Items below the waist are considered contaminated.'),q('Which confirms that sterilization occurred?','A biological indicator','A clean appearance','A warm pack','A dry wrapper','Spore tests confirm sterility.'),q('OSHA requires employers to offer free:','Hepatitis B vaccination to at-risk employees','Influenza vaccination to all patients','Gym memberships','Annual vacations','Part of the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the difference between medical and surgical asepsis, how instruments are made sterile and verified, and how to apply sterile gloves following HOSA Skill VIII.',rubric:[['Medical vs surgical asepsis',['medical asepsis','surgical asepsis','reduce','eliminate','sterile field']],['Processing and sterilization',['sanitiz','disinfect','autoclave','steam','pressure','indicator','spore']],['Skill VIII technique',['rings','cuff','fold','dominant','under the cuff','above waist','inspect']],['Removal and safety',['inside out','dispose','hand hygiene','contaminated','start over']]],model:'Medical asepsis reduces the number and spread of microorganisms through hand hygiene, clean gloves and surface cleaning, while surgical asepsis eliminates all microorganisms and is used for procedures that enter sterile tissue, with rules such as a 1-inch contaminated border, keeping sterile items above waist level and never turning away from the field. Instruments are first sanitized by scrubbing with detergent, rinsed and dried, wrapped or pouched with hinges open and indicators inside and out, then sterilized in an autoclave with steam under pressure, commonly about 250°F at 15 psi for 20–30 minutes; chemical indicators show conditions were reached and weekly biological spore tests confirm sterility, and packs are dated and stored dry. For sterile gloving, I remove rings and my watch and wash for surgical asepsis, open the package on a clean surface with cuffs toward me, grasp the dominant-hand glove by the cuff fold with my non-dominant hand and pull it on keeping the cuff turned back, slide my gloved fingers under the cuff of the second glove, insert my other hand and ease it on without touching my gloved thumb to the contaminated cuff, then smooth and inspect the gloves and keep my hands above my waist touching only sterile items. To remove them, I pull each glove off inside out without touching the outside, dispose of them and perform hand hygiene; if anything touches a non-sterile surface, I start over.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HH,OSHA_BBP,CDC_STERIL]};

export const M7={title:'Medical terminology, anatomy and pharmacology basics',hook:'“Cholecystectomy,” “tachycardia,” “q.i.d.”—medical language is precise and compact. Anatomy and terminology are 10% of the test, and every clinical task, from charting to medication administration, depends on reading and using these terms correctly.',objectives:['Build and decode medical terms from prefixes, roots and suffixes.','Use directional terms, body planes, cavities and regions.','Relate major body systems to common disorders seen in the medical office.','Explain medication routes, rights, abbreviations, drug schedules and simple dose calculations.'],sections:[
['Building medical words',[
'Most medical terms combine a **prefix** (beginning), a **word root** (foundation) and a **suffix** (ending), often joined by a **combining vowel** (usually o). Read terms from the **suffix**, then the beginning: **gastr/o/enter/itis** is inflammation (-itis) of the stomach (gastr) and intestines (enter). Common prefixes: **brady-** (slow), **tachy-** (fast), **hyper-** (above), **hypo-** (below), **dys-** (difficult or painful), **a-/an-** (without). Common suffixes: **-ectomy** (surgical removal), **-otomy** (cutting into), **-ostomy** (creating an opening), **-itis** (inflammation), **-algia** (pain), **-scopy** (visual examination), **-gram** (record) and **-graph** (instrument for recording).',
{t:'w',kind:'termlab',title:'Take medical words apart',words:[{word:'tachycardia',parts:['tachy-','cardi','-ia'],meanings:['fast','heart','condition'],meaning:'A condition of a fast heart rate, generally above 100 beats per minute in adults.'},{word:'cholecystectomy',parts:['','cholecyst','-ectomy'],meanings:['no prefix','gallbladder','surgical removal'],meaning:'Surgical removal of the gallbladder.'},{word:'hypoglycemia',parts:['hypo-','glyc','-emia'],meanings:['below normal','sugar','blood condition'],meaning:'A condition of low blood sugar.'},{word:'dysuria',parts:['dys-','ur','-ia'],meanings:['painful or difficult','urine','condition'],meaning:'Painful or difficult urination, often a symptom of urinary tract infection.'},{word:'arthroscopy',parts:['','arthr/o','-scopy'],meanings:['no prefix','joint','visual examination'],meaning:'Visual examination of the inside of a joint with a scope.'}]}]],
['Body organization and directional terms',[
'Anatomical position is standing upright, facing forward, arms at the sides with palms forward. **Directional terms**: **superior** (toward the head) and **inferior** (toward the feet); **anterior (ventral)** and **posterior (dorsal)**; **medial** (toward the midline) and **lateral** (away from it); **proximal** (closer to the point of attachment) and **distal** (farther away); **superficial** and **deep**. **Body planes**: the **sagittal** plane divides right and left (the **midsagittal** plane into equal halves), the **frontal (coronal)** plane anterior and posterior, and the **transverse** plane superior and inferior. Body cavities include the **cranial**, **spinal**, **thoracic**, **abdominal** and **pelvic** cavities. The abdomen is divided into **four quadrants**—right upper (RUQ), left upper (LUQ), right lower (RLQ) and left lower (LLQ)—or **nine regions**, such as the epigastric and umbilical regions.',
match('Match the directional term to its meaning',[['Proximal','Closer to the point of attachment'],['Distal','Farther from the point of attachment'],['Medial','Toward the midline'],['Lateral','Away from the midline'],['Superior','Toward the head'],['Posterior','Toward the back']])]],
['Body systems in the medical office',[
'Medical assistants see common disorders across systems. **Cardiovascular**: hypertension, coronary artery disease, heart failure. **Respiratory**: asthma, COPD, pneumonia. **Endocrine**: diabetes mellitus and thyroid disorders. **Musculoskeletal**: arthritis, osteoporosis, sprains and fractures. **Integumentary**: dermatitis, acne, skin cancers. **Digestive**: GERD, gastroenteritis, irritable bowel syndrome. **Urinary**: urinary tract infection, kidney stones. **Nervous**: migraine, stroke, seizures. **Reproductive**: menstrual disorders, sexually transmitted infections. **Immune and lymphatic**: allergies and HIV. **Sensory**: cataracts, glaucoma, otitis media. Knowing the organ and its function helps you understand why a provider orders a test—for example, an A1C for diabetes or a urinalysis for a suspected UTI.',
match('Match the disorder to its body system',[['Hypertension','Cardiovascular'],['Asthma','Respiratory'],['Diabetes mellitus','Endocrine'],['Osteoporosis','Musculoskeletal'],['Urinary tract infection','Urinary'],['Otitis media','Sensory (ear)']])]],
['Pharmacology basics',[
'Medications are known by a **chemical**, **generic** (official) and **brand (trade)** name. They are given by routes such as **oral**, **sublingual**, **buccal**, **topical**, **transdermal**, **inhalation**, **rectal** and **parenteral** injections: **intradermal** (10–15°), **subcutaneous** (45–90°) and **intramuscular** (90°). Before giving any medication—only as allowed by state law and under a provider’s order—medical assistants follow the **rights of medication administration**: right **patient**, **drug**, **dose**, **route**, **time**, **technique** and **documentation** (some texts add reason and the right to refuse), checking the label **three times**. Prescription abbreviations include **b.i.d.** (twice a day), **t.i.d.** (three times a day), **q.i.d.** (four times a day), **p.o.** (by mouth), **p.r.n.** (as needed) and **NPO** (nothing by mouth); error-prone abbreviations such as “U” for units and “QD” are avoided. The **DEA** classifies **controlled substances** in **Schedules I–V**, from highest to lowest abuse potential.',
{t:'w',kind:'dosecalc',title:'Dose calculation practice',types:['tablets','liquid','convert'],prompt:'Set up each problem with units (desired ÷ have × quantity), then check.'},
match('Match the abbreviation to its meaning',[['b.i.d.','Twice a day'],['t.i.d.','Three times a day'],['q.i.d.','Four times a day'],['p.o.','By mouth'],['p.r.n.','As needed'],['NPO','Nothing by mouth']])]],
['Applying terminology in the chart',[
kase('Reading an order',{a:{text:'An order reads: “Amoxicillin 500 mg p.o. t.i.d. × 10 days.” What does it mean?',choices:[['500 mg by mouth three times a day for 10 days','b','Correct. p.o. = by mouth; t.i.d. = three times a day.',true],['500 mg intramuscularly four times a day','b','p.o. means by mouth and t.i.d. means three times a day.',false]]},b:{text:'The capsules come as 250 mg each. How many capsules per dose?',choices:[['2 capsules (500 mg ÷ 250 mg)','c','Correct. Desired ÷ have × quantity.',true],['5 capsules','c','500 ÷ 250 = 2.',false]]},c:{text:'You confirm the patient has no penicillin allergy, review the rights and document after the provider’s instructions are followed.',end:true}})]],
["Giving injections safely",["Where state law and office policy allow, medical assistants administer injections and vaccines under a provider’s order. **Intramuscular (IM)** sites include the **deltoid** (adults and older children, small volumes), the **vastus lateralis** of the thigh (the preferred site for **infants**) and the **ventrogluteal** site; needles are commonly 22–25 gauge and 1–1½ inches long for adults, chosen by patient size. **Subcutaneous (SC)** injections use short 25–27 gauge needles at 45–90° in the upper outer arm, abdomen or thigh. **Intradermal (ID)** injections, such as the tuberculin skin test, use 26–27 gauge needles at 10–15° with the bevel up, producing a small wheal. Needle **gauge** is inverse to diameter: a higher number is thinner.","Preparation includes checking the order and the rights, verifying allergies, reading the label three times and checking expiration, cleaning the vial top with alcohol, injecting air equal to the dose and withdrawing the medication, and using a **filter needle** for glass ampules. The site is cleaned with alcohol and allowed to dry. After injection, the needle’s safety device is activated immediately and the sharp is placed in a sharps container—**never recap** a used needle. Patients are observed for reactions—often **15 minutes** after vaccines—and the medication, dose, route, site, lot number, time and patient response are documented, along with the VIS given for vaccines.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the injection type to its details","pairs":[["Intramuscular","90° into muscle such as the deltoid"],["Subcutaneous","45–90° into fatty tissue"],["Intradermal","10–15° bevel up, forming a wheal"],["Vastus lateralis","Preferred IM site for infants"],["Filter needle","Used to withdraw from a glass ampule"],["Higher gauge number","Thinner needle"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Decode terms suffix first: prefix + root + suffix (e.g., -ectomy removal, -itis inflammation, -scopy visual exam).','Directional terms (proximal/distal, medial/lateral), planes (sagittal, frontal, transverse) and four abdominal quadrants.','Medication rights (patient, drug, dose, route, time, technique, documentation), routes and angles, abbreviations (b.i.d., t.i.d., q.i.d., p.o., p.r.n., NPO) and DEA Schedules I–V.'],terms:[['Word root','Foundation of a medical term.'],['Proximal','Closer to the point of attachment.'],['Controlled substance','Drug regulated by the DEA because of abuse potential.']],mcqs:[q('The suffix -ectomy means:','Surgical removal','Inflammation','Visual examination','Cutting into','Appendectomy is removal of the appendix.'),q('Tachycardia means:','A fast heart rate','A slow heart rate','An enlarged heart','Heart inflammation','Tachy- means fast.'),q('Which term means toward the midline?','Medial','Lateral','Distal','Superficial','Lateral is away from the midline.'),q('The sagittal plane divides the body into:','Right and left portions','Front and back','Top and bottom','Inside and outside','The midsagittal plane makes equal halves.'),q('q.i.d. means:','Four times a day','Every day','Twice a day','At bedtime','t.i.d. is three times a day.'),q('An intramuscular injection is given at:','90°','15°','45° only','5°','Intradermal is 10–15°.'),q('Which schedule has the highest abuse potential and no accepted medical use?','Schedule I','Schedule V','Schedule III','Schedule II','Schedule V has the lowest.'),q('An order for 500 mg when tablets are 250 mg requires:','2 tablets','5 tablets','1 tablet','0.5 tablet','Desired ÷ have.')],frq:{prompt:'A provider orders “acetaminophen 650 mg p.o. q6h p.r.n. pain.” Explain the order, calculate the dose using 325-mg tablets, and describe the safety checks you would perform before the medication is given.',rubric:[['Interpreting the order',['by mouth','every 6 hours','as needed','pain','p.o.','p.r.n.']],['Dose calculation',['2 tablets','650','325','desired','have']],['Medication rights',['right patient','drug','dose','route','time','documentation']],['Safety checks',['allerg','label','three times','identify','scope','provider']]],model:'The order means acetaminophen 650 mg by mouth (p.o.) every 6 hours as needed (p.r.n.) for pain. Using desired ÷ have × quantity, 650 mg ÷ 325 mg × 1 tablet = 2 tablets per dose. Before the medication is given—only if state law and office policy allow me to administer it under the provider’s order—I would follow the rights of medication administration: identify the right patient with two identifiers, confirm the right drug, dose, route and time, use the right technique and document correctly. I would check the medication label three times, verify the expiration date, ask about allergies and when the patient last took any acetaminophen-containing products to avoid exceeding the daily limit, and ask the provider about anything unclear. After the patient takes the medication, I would document the drug, dose, route, time and the patient’s response.'},sources:[RULES,MEDWORDS,OS_TERMS,DEA]};

export const M8={title:'Health histories, vital signs and the physical examination (Skills III and V)',hook:'Before the provider walks in, the medical assistant has already gathered the story—the chief complaint, medications, allergies and history—and prepared the room and patient. Physical examinations are 10% of the test, and Skills III and V test the history and exam preparation.',objectives:['Obtain and record a complete health history following Skill III.','Measure vital signs, height, weight and BMI accurately.','Describe examination methods, positions and instruments.','Prepare and assist with a routine physical examination following Skill V.'],sections:[
['The health history',[
'A health history usually includes **identifying data**; the **chief complaint (CC)**—the reason for the visit, in the patient’s own words; the **history of present illness (HPI)**, using details such as onset, location, duration, character, aggravating and relieving factors and severity; the **past medical history (PMH)**—illnesses, surgeries, hospitalizations, immunizations; **medications** with doses and reasons; **allergies** and reactions; **family history (FH)**; **social history (SH)**—tobacco, alcohol, drugs, caffeine, occupation, exercise; and a **review of systems (ROS)**. This information is **subjective**.',
'In Skill III (8 minutes), the competitor obtains a blank paper or electronic history form, **escorts the patient to a comfortable, private area**, sits **1.5 to 4 feet** from the patient, explains the purpose and that the information is **confidential**, and records **name, date, date of birth, age, date of last physical and occupation**; the **chief complaint and its characteristics**; **all medications** with dosages and reasons; and **allergies**. They ask **every symptoms question** and **every diseases and conditions question**, **expand on each “yes”** answer, include all hospitalizations and surgeries, expand on yes responses in the family and social history, then **summarize and clarify** with the patient, write a **summary** in the chart that highlights significant findings, assemble the forms for the provider, thank the patient and explain the next step and any wait. They speak clearly, give the patient time to answer, **explain terms** the patient might not understand and stay on topic (47 points; 70% is 32.9).',
order('Obtain a health history (HOSA Skill III)',['Get the form and escort the patient to a private area','Sit 1.5–4 feet away; explain purpose and confidentiality','Record identifying data and occupation','Record the chief complaint and its characteristics','Record medications (with doses and reasons) and allergies','Ask every symptom and disease question; expand on “yes” answers','Complete hospitalizations, surgeries, family and social history','Summarize, clarify, chart a summary and explain the next step'])]],
['Vital signs and measurements',[
'Medical assistants measure **temperature**, **pulse**, **respirations**, **blood pressure**, often **oxygen saturation** and **pain**, plus **height and weight**. Adult teaching ranges include a pulse of **60–100**, respirations of **12–20** and blood pressure below **120/80 mm Hg**; temperatures vary by site (oral about 97.6–99.6°F). Blood pressure technique: correct **cuff size**, bare arm at heart level, patient seated with feet flat after resting, inflate about 30 mm Hg above the point where the radial pulse disappears, then deflate slowly (2–3 mm Hg per second), recording the **first sound (systolic)** and the **disappearance of sound (diastolic)**. **Body mass index (BMI)** = weight (kg) ÷ height (m)², or weight (lb) × 703 ÷ height (in)²; adult categories: below 18.5 underweight, 18.5–24.9 healthy weight, 25–29.9 overweight and 30 or higher obesity.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Record or report?',prompt:'Decide which readings are within the usual adult teaching ranges and which you would report to the provider.',person:'patient',reportTo:'provider'},
{t:'example',title:'Calculate BMI',prompt:'A patient weighs 180 lb and is 5 feet 10 inches tall. What is the BMI and category?',steps:['Height = 70 inches; height² = 4,900.','BMI = 180 × 703 ÷ 4,900.','126,540 ÷ 4,900 ≈ 25.8.'],answer:'BMI ≈ 25.8, in the overweight category (25–29.9). (Values are invented for practice.)'}]],
['Examination methods, positions and instruments',[
'Providers examine using **inspection** (looking), **palpation** (feeling), **percussion** (tapping to assess underlying structures), **auscultation** (listening, usually with a stethoscope), **mensuration** (measuring) and **manipulation** (moving joints). Positions include **horizontal recumbent (supine)**—lying flat on the back; **dorsal recumbent**—on the back with knees bent and feet flat; **Fowler’s** (head raised about 45–60°) and **semi-Fowler’s**; **prone** (face down); **Sims’** (left side with the right knee bent); **lithotomy** (on the back with feet in stirrups for pelvic exams); and **knee-chest**. Instruments include the **otoscope** (ears), **ophthalmoscope** (eyes), **percussion (reflex) hammer**, **tuning fork** (hearing and vibration), **nasal and vaginal specula**, **tongue depressor**, **penlight**, **tape measure** and **stethoscope**. Equipment is laid out **in order of use**.',
match('Match the position to its use',[['Horizontal recumbent (supine)','Examination of the front of the body'],['Lithotomy','Pelvic examination'],['Sims’','Rectal examination or enema'],['Fowler’s','Patients with breathing difficulty'],['Prone','Examination of the back and spine'],['Dorsal recumbent','Supine with knees bent for comfort or some exams']]),
match('Match the instrument to its purpose',[['Otoscope','Examines the ear canal and eardrum'],['Ophthalmoscope','Examines the interior of the eye'],['Percussion hammer','Tests deep tendon reflexes'],['Tuning fork','Tests hearing and vibration sense'],['Tongue depressor','Holds the tongue to view the throat'],['Speculum','Opens a body cavity for inspection']])]],
['Preparing and assisting with a physical exam (Skill V)',[
'In Skill V (6 minutes), the competitor **assesses and prepares the exam room**, **reviews the chart** for the completed history and physical form, performs hand hygiene and **prepares the equipment** listed in the scenario on the **Mayo tray or counter in order of use, covered with a towel**. They pull out the table step and place a **gown and drape** on the table. Calling the patient in, they **greet the patient by name**, introduce themselves and give instructions, **verbalize measuring vital signs, height and weight**, instruct the patient to **empty the bladder** to provide a urine specimen in a **labeled container** left at the marked door, then instruct the patient to remove outer clothing, put on the **gown with the opening in the back**, sit at the end of the table and cover the legs with the drape. When the patient is ready, they notify the physician. When directed, they position the patient in **horizontal recumbent position** with the head on a small pillow, arms at the sides, legs slightly apart and the drape loose at the sides. After the exam, they help the patient **sit up slowly, watching for dizziness**, instruct or help the patient to dress, give the provider’s instructions, answer questions and see the patient out. To **clean the room**, they put on gloves, wrap the table paper and discard disposables, **disinfect** the tabletops and table, discard the gloves, **replace** supplies and paper and perform hand hygiene (33 points; 70% is 23.1).',
order('Assist with a routine physical (HOSA Skill V)',['Prepare the room and review the chart','Hand hygiene; set equipment in order of use and cover it','Place a gown and drape on the table','Greet the patient, measure vitals, height and weight','Have the patient void into a labeled specimen container','Gown with the opening in back and drape; notify the provider','Position horizontal recumbent when directed','Sit the patient up slowly, give instructions and clean the room'])]],
['Applying exam-room judgment',[
kase('Dizziness after the exam',{a:{text:'After the exam, an older patient sits up and says the room is spinning. What should you do?',choices:[['Help the patient lie back or stay seated, stay with them and notify the provider','b','Correct. Postural hypotension can cause falls.',true],['Ask the patient to stand and walk it off','b','Standing could cause a fall.',false]]},b:{text:'The dizziness resolves after a few minutes. What next?',choices:[['Assist the patient to dress, document the episode and give the provider’s instructions','c','Correct. Document and follow up.',true],['Leave the patient alone to dress','c','Stay nearby until the patient is steady.',false]]},c:{text:'You document the episode and the provider rechecks orthostatic blood pressures before the patient leaves.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Health history: CC, HPI, PMH, medications, allergies, FH, SH and ROS; Skill III: private area, 1.5–4 feet, confidentiality, expand on every “yes,” summarize and clarify.','Vitals: pulse 60–100, respirations 12–20, BP below 120/80; BMI = lb × 703 ÷ in²; 25–29.9 overweight.','Skill V: equipment in order of use and covered, gown opening in back, labeled urine specimen, horizontal recumbent when directed, sit up slowly, disinfect and restock.'],terms:[['Chief complaint','The main reason for the visit in the patient’s words.'],['Auscultation','Listening to body sounds, usually with a stethoscope.'],['Horizontal recumbent','Lying flat on the back.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill III, the appropriate distance from the patient during the interview is:','1.5 to 4 feet','6 to 10 feet','Less than 6 inches','12 feet','Personal space supports comfort.'),q('The reason for the visit in the patient’s own words is the:','Chief complaint','Review of systems','Assessment','Plan','It is subjective information.'),q('Listening to body sounds with a stethoscope is:','Auscultation','Palpation','Percussion','Inspection','Percussion is tapping.'),q('In Skill V, the patient is positioned in:','Horizontal recumbent position','Lithotomy','Prone','Knee-chest','As directed by the judge.'),q('The gown is worn with the opening:','In the back','In the front','On the side','It does not matter','The rating sheet specifies the back.'),q('Equipment for the exam is arranged:','In order of use and covered with a towel','Randomly','In a drawer','On the floor','Order of use speeds the exam.'),q('A BMI of 27 is classified as:','Overweight','Underweight','Healthy weight','Obesity','25–29.9 is overweight.'),q('Which position is used for pelvic examinations?','Lithotomy','Sims’','Prone','Fowler’s','Feet are in stirrups.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would prepare the room and patient for a routine physical examination following HOSA Skill V, and how you would obtain the health history beforehand following Skill III.',rubric:[['History setup and content',['private','1.5','confidential','chief complaint','medications','allergies']],['History technique',['every','expand','yes','summarize','clarify','explain terms']],['Room and patient preparation',['order of use','towel','gown','drape','vital','urine','labeled']],['Positioning and cleanup',['horizontal recumbent','pillow','dizziness','disinfect','replace','hand hygiene']]],model:'For the history, I would take the patient to a comfortable, private area, sit 1.5 to 4 feet away and explain that the information is confidential and helps the provider. I would record identifying data, date of last physical and occupation, the chief complaint and its characteristics, all medications with doses and reasons and allergies, ask every symptom and disease question and expand on each yes answer, and complete hospitalizations, surgeries and family and social history. I would summarize and clarify with the patient, explain unfamiliar terms, write a summary highlighting significant findings and have the forms ready for the provider. For the exam, I would prepare the room, review the chart, perform hand hygiene and set the scenario’s equipment on the Mayo tray in order of use, covered with a towel, with a gown and drape on the table. I would greet the patient, measure vital signs, height and weight, have the patient void into a labeled container, instruct them to put on the gown with the opening in back, sit at the end of the table and cover their legs, then notify the provider. When directed, I would position them horizontal recumbent with a small pillow, arms at the sides and the drape loose. Afterward I would help them sit up slowly while watching for dizziness, give the provider’s instructions, see them out and clean by discarding paper and disposables with gloves, disinfecting surfaces, replacing supplies and performing hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_VITALS,CDC_BMI]};

export const M9={title:'Collecting and processing specimens; urinalysis (Skill VII)',hook:'A urine specimen left at room temperature for hours, a blood tube drawn in the wrong order or a label written at the desk instead of the bedside can all produce wrong results. Collecting and processing specimens is 10% of the test, and Skill VII asks you to test urine with a reagent strip and time each pad precisely.',objectives:['Explain CLIA test categories, quality control and specimen labeling.','Describe venipuncture, the order of draw and capillary puncture.','Collect urine specimens correctly and describe normal urinalysis results.','Test urine with a reagent strip following Skill VII.'],sections:[
['Laboratory regulation and quality',[
'The **Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA)** regulate laboratory testing on human specimens. Tests are categorized by complexity: **waived** tests are simple, with low risk of error—urine reagent strips, urine pregnancy tests, blood glucose meters, rapid strep and hemoglobin—and are commonly performed in medical offices with a CLIA Certificate of Waiver; **moderate-** and **high-complexity** tests require more training and oversight. Quality depends on following the **manufacturer’s instructions**, running **quality control (QC)** with known controls as required, checking **expiration dates** and storage, calibrating equipment and documenting results. Every specimen is **labeled at the patient’s side** with at least **two identifiers**, the date and time and the collector’s initials, and paired with a requisition.',
sort('CLIA-waived test or not usually waived?',['Commonly waived in offices','More complex (not waived)'],[['Urine reagent strip',0],['Urine pregnancy test',0],['Blood glucose meter',0],['Rapid strep test',0],['Blood culture identification',1],['Complete microscopic tissue biopsy analysis',1]])]],
['Blood collection',[
'**Venipuncture** collects blood from a vein, most often the **median cubital** vein in the antecubital area. The phlebotomist identifies the patient, checks requisitions, assembles equipment, applies a **tourniquet** for no more than about **one minute**, cleans the site with 70% alcohol and lets it dry, anchors the vein, inserts the needle bevel up at about **15–30°**, fills tubes in the correct **order of draw**, releases the tourniquet, withdraws the needle, activates the safety device, applies pressure and labels tubes at the patient’s side. The **CLSI order of draw** for evacuated tubes prevents additive carryover: **blood cultures (sterile)** → **light blue** (sodium citrate, coagulation) → **red, gold or tiger-top** (serum, with or without clot activator or gel) → **green** (heparin) → **lavender** (EDTA, hematology) → **gray** (sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate, glucose). Tubes with additives are gently **inverted**. **Capillary puncture** of a fingertip (or heel in infants) is used for small volumes; the first drop is wiped away when the method requires, and excessive squeezing is avoided because it dilutes the sample with tissue fluid.',
order('Order of draw for evacuated tubes',['Blood culture bottles (sterile)','Light blue: sodium citrate','Red, gold or tiger-top: serum tubes','Green: heparin','Lavender: EDTA','Gray: sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate'])]],
['Urine collection',[
'Urine specimen types include a **random** specimen (any time), a **first morning** specimen (most concentrated—best for pregnancy tests and detecting protein), a **clean-catch midstream** specimen for **culture** (the patient cleanses the urinary opening, begins voiding into the toilet, collects the middle portion in a sterile container and finishes in the toilet), a **timed or 24-hour** specimen (the first morning void is discarded, then all urine for 24 hours is collected, ending with the next morning’s first void, kept refrigerated or with preservative) and **catheterized** specimens. Specimens should be tested within **one hour** or refrigerated, because at room temperature bacteria multiply, pH rises, glucose decreases and cells and casts break down. **Chain of custody** forms document every handler for drug testing.',
match('Match the urine specimen to its use',[['First morning specimen','Most concentrated; pregnancy testing'],['Clean-catch midstream','Urine culture'],['24-hour specimen','Measuring substances excreted over a day'],['Random specimen','Routine screening at any time'],['Chain of custody specimen','Drug testing with documented handling'],['Refrigeration','Preserves a specimen that cannot be tested within an hour']])]],
['Urinalysis and reagent strips',[
'A **routine urinalysis** includes **physical** examination (color, clarity, odor, specific gravity), **chemical** testing with reagent strips and sometimes **microscopic** examination of sediment. Normal urine is pale yellow to amber and clear. Reagent strips have pads that change color for parameters such as **glucose**, **bilirubin**, **ketones**, **specific gravity**, **blood**, **pH**, **protein**, **urobilinogen**, **nitrite** and **leukocytes**; each pad is read at its own **timed interval** printed on the bottle. Normal findings include negative glucose, bilirubin, ketones, blood, protein, nitrite and leukocytes, a pH of about **4.5–8.0** and specific gravity of about **1.005–1.030**. **Positive nitrite and leukocytes** suggest a urinary tract infection; **glucose and ketones** may indicate uncontrolled diabetes; **protein** may indicate kidney disease. Medical assistants record results; the provider interprets them.',
sort('Normal or abnormal urine finding?',['Usually normal','Abnormal—record for the provider'],[['Negative glucose',0],['pH 6.0',0],['Specific gravity 1.020',0],['Positive nitrite',1],['Large leukocytes',1],['Moderate ketones',1],['Pale yellow and clear',0],['Positive blood',1]])]],
['Testing urine with a reagent strip (Skill VII)',[
'In Skill VII (6 minutes), the competitor assembles equipment, performs hand hygiene, dons **non-latex gloves** and other PPE, and **verifies that the name on the specimen container matches the laboratory report form**. They **gently rotate the container** between their hands to mix the urine, **hold the strip by the clear end**, **immerse it so all reagent areas are submerged**, **remove it immediately** and **tap the edge** against the container to remove excess urine. They turn the strip so the pads face them, **hold it horizontally** near the color chart on the bottle (holding it horizontally prevents runover between pads) and use their **watch** to read each pad at the **correct time interval**, recording all results on the laboratory report. They place the strip on a paper towel for the judge to verify, discard the strip and contaminated supplies, verbalize discarding the specimen per protocol, clean the work area with disinfectant, remove PPE, perform hand hygiene and **record the results for each section in the patient’s chart** (33 points; 70% is 23.1).',
order('Test urine with a reagent strip (HOSA Skill VII)',['Assemble supplies, hand hygiene, gloves and PPE','Verify the specimen name matches the lab report','Gently rotate the container to mix','Hold the strip by the clear end and immerse all pads','Remove immediately and tap off excess urine','Hold horizontally next to the color chart','Read each pad at its timed interval with your watch','Discard, disinfect, remove PPE, hand hygiene and chart results']),
kase('A timing problem',{a:{text:'You dip the strip and then answer a phone call, reading the strip four minutes later. What is the problem?',choices:[['Pads read after their timed intervals give inaccurate results; repeat with a new strip','b','Correct. Each pad must be read at its specified time.',true],['No problem—colors are stable for hours','b','Colors change over time, causing false results.',false]]},b:{text:'The specimen was collected three hours ago and left on the counter. What should you do?',choices:[['Note that it was not tested within an hour or refrigerated and request a new specimen per policy','c','Correct. Old specimens give unreliable results.',true],['Test it anyway without comment','c','Bacteria and chemical changes alter results.',false]]},c:{text:'You obtain a fresh specimen, test it with precise timing and record each result.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['CLIA-waived tests (reagent strips, pregnancy, glucose, rapid strep) still require following instructions and QC; label specimens at the patient’s side with two identifiers.','Order of draw: blood cultures, light blue, red/gold, green, lavender, gray; tourniquet no more than about a minute.','Test urine within an hour or refrigerate; Skill VII: verify names, mix, hold by the clear end, dip all pads, tap, hold horizontally and read each pad at its timed interval.'],terms:[['CLIA-waived test','Simple test with low risk of error approved for waived settings.'],['Order of draw','Sequence of tube collection that prevents additive carryover.'],['Clean-catch midstream','Urine collected after cleansing, from the middle of the stream.']],mcqs:[q('Which tube is drawn first after blood cultures?','Light blue (sodium citrate)','Lavender (EDTA)','Gray (fluoride)','Green (heparin)','Order of draw prevents carryover.'),q('A lavender-top tube contains:','EDTA','Sodium citrate','Heparin','Sodium fluoride','Used for hematology.'),q('Which urine specimen is best for a culture?','Clean-catch midstream','Random','24-hour','First morning without cleansing','It minimizes contamination.'),q('Urine should be tested within about:','One hour or refrigerated','24 hours at room temperature','One week','It does not matter','Changes occur at room temperature.'),q('In Skill VII, the reagent strip is held:','By the clear end','By the reagent pads','With bare fingers on the pads','Vertically while reading','Avoid touching the pads.'),q('Why is the strip held horizontally while reading?','To prevent urine running between pads','To dry it faster','To keep it warm','It is not necessary','Runover mixes chemicals.'),q('Positive nitrite and leukocytes suggest:','A urinary tract infection','Normal urine','Dehydration only','Pregnancy','The provider interprets results.'),q('A urine pregnancy test is a:','CLIA-waived test','High-complexity test','Microscopic test','Culture','Commonly done in offices.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would collect a clean-catch urine specimen and test it with a reagent strip following HOSA Skill VII, including how you ensure accurate results.',rubric:[['Clean-catch instructions',['cleanse','midstream','sterile','container','label']],['Specimen handling',['one hour','refrigerat','identif','two identifiers','match']],['Skill VII technique',['rotate','clear end','immerse','tap','horizontal','timed','watch']],['Cleanup and documentation',['discard','disinfect','gloves','hand hygiene','record','chart']]],model:'I would give the patient a labeled sterile container and explain the clean-catch method: wash hands, cleanse the urinary opening, begin voiding into the toilet, collect the middle portion of the stream in the container without touching the inside and finish voiding in the toilet, then close the lid. The specimen would be tested within one hour or refrigerated. For Skill VII, I would assemble supplies, perform hand hygiene, put on non-latex gloves and PPE and verify that the name on the container matches the laboratory report. I would gently rotate the container to mix, hold the strip by the clear end, immerse it so all pads are covered, remove it immediately and tap the edge to remove excess urine, then hold it horizontally next to the color chart and use my watch to read each pad at its specified time, recording every result. Accuracy depends on fresh, correctly labeled specimens, unexpired strips stored properly, quality control as required and precise timing. Finally, I would discard the strip and specimen per protocol, disinfect the work area, remove PPE, perform hand hygiene and record each result in the patient’s chart for the provider to interpret.'},sources:[RULES,CMS_CLIA,MEDLINE_UA]};

export const M10={title:'Diagnostic testing, vision screening and pediatric measurements (Skills IV and VI)',hook:'An ECG strip, a spirometry curve, a Snellen chart and a measuring tape around an infant’s head each turn the body into data the provider can act on. Diagnostic testing and clinical procedures make up 20% of the test, and Skills IV and VI test two of the most common screenings.',objectives:['Perform a 12-lead ECG with correct electrode placement and recognize artifacts.','Describe spirometry, audiometry and other office diagnostic tests.','Screen distance and near visual acuity following Skill VI.','Measure an infant’s or child’s head and chest circumference following Skill IV and use growth charts.'],sections:[
['Electrocardiography',[
'An **electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG)** records the heart’s electrical activity. A standard **12-lead ECG** uses **10 electrodes**: four **limb** electrodes (right arm, left arm, right leg, left leg) and six **chest (precordial)** electrodes: **V1** at the fourth intercostal space at the right sternal border, **V2** at the fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border, **V3** midway between V2 and V4, **V4** at the fifth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line, **V5** at the left anterior axillary line level with V4 and **V6** at the left midaxillary line level with V4. The **P wave** shows atrial depolarization, the **QRS complex** ventricular depolarization and the **T wave** ventricular repolarization. Standard paper speed is **25 mm/second**. **Artifacts** come from patient movement or tremors (somatic), **AC (60-cycle) electrical interference**, a **wandering baseline** from poor contact or lotion and interrupted baselines from loose leads. Prepare skin, ask the patient to lie still and breathe normally and keep cables untangled. A **Holter monitor** records continuously for 24–48 hours.',
match('Match the chest lead to its position',[['V1','Fourth intercostal space, right sternal border'],['V2','Fourth intercostal space, left sternal border'],['V3','Midway between V2 and V4'],['V4','Fifth intercostal space, left midclavicular line'],['V5','Left anterior axillary line, level with V4'],['V6','Left midaxillary line, level with V4']]),
{t:'w',kind:'ecg',title:'Recognize rhythms and artifacts',prompt:'Compare normal sinus rhythm with common abnormal rhythms and artifacts. Which would you report to the provider right away?'}]],
['Other diagnostic tests',[
'**Spirometry** measures lung function: the patient takes the deepest breath possible and blows out as hard and fast as possible into a mouthpiece, measuring **forced vital capacity (FVC)** and **forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV₁)**; good coaching and at least three acceptable efforts are needed. **Peak flow meters** help patients with asthma monitor airflow at home. **Audiometry** tests hearing at different frequencies with headphones, and **tympanometry** assesses the middle ear. **Pulse oximetry** estimates oxygen saturation. Point-of-care tests include **blood glucose** and **hemoglobin A1C**, **cholesterol**, **hemoglobin**, rapid **strep**, **influenza** and **COVID-19**, **pregnancy** and **occult blood** tests. Imaging such as X-ray, ultrasound, CT and MRI is usually performed elsewhere, and medical assistants give patients **preparation instructions**—for example, fasting before certain blood tests or a full bladder for some pelvic ultrasounds.',
sort('Which system does the test evaluate?',['Respiratory','Cardiovascular','Sensory'],[['Spirometry',0],['Peak flow measurement',0],['12-lead ECG',1],['Holter monitor',1],['Audiometry',2],['Snellen chart',2]])]],
['Screening visual acuity (Skill VI)',[
'**Distance vision** is screened with a **Snellen chart** at **20 feet**. Results are recorded as a fraction: **20/40** means the patient reads at 20 feet what a person with normal vision reads at 40 feet. **Near vision** uses a **Jaeger card** held about **14–16 inches** from the eyes, and **color vision** is screened with **Ishihara** plates. In Skill VI (5 minutes), the competitor performs hand hygiene, greets and identifies the patient, notes whether the patient wears **glasses** or asks about **contact lenses**, and explains that the patient should read each line as it is pointed to and **keep both eyes open while covering one eye**. The patient stands **20 feet** from the chart and reads with both eyes open; then **covers the left eye** with an occluder and reads with the **right eye**, using corrective lenses as needed; the competitor **records the smallest line read with one or fewer mistakes**; the patient then covers the right eye and reads with the left, and the result is recorded. The competitor records observations such as **squinting or turning the head**. For near vision, the patient sits up straight in a well-lighted area and holds the **Jaeger chart 14–16 inches** away, reading paragraphs aloud with both eyes open, first **with** and then **without** corrective lenses. Results and problems are recorded, the patient is thanked, supplies are cleaned and hand hygiene is performed (39 points; 70% is 27.3).',
label('eye','Structures involved in vision',[['cornea','Cornea','Clear front surface that bends light.'],['lens','Lens','Focuses light; stiffens with age (presbyopia).'],['retina','Retina','Light-sensing layer.'],['macula','Macula','Central sharp vision used in acuity testing.'],['optic','Optic nerve','Carries signals to the brain.']]),
chk('In Skill VI, a patient reads the 20/30 line with one mistake but misses three letters on the 20/25 line. What do you record?',['20/30','20/25','20/20','20/40'],'Record the smallest line read with one or fewer mistakes.')]],
['Pediatric measurements (Skill IV)',[
'**Head circumference** is measured at well-child visits, usually until about age 2–3, to track brain growth; **chest circumference** may also be measured. Measurements are plotted on **growth charts** (WHO charts are recommended for children under 2 and CDC charts for ages 2 and older) to compare with percentiles and follow trends. In Skill IV (5 minutes), the competitor performs hand hygiene, greets and identifies the patient and parent, explains in understandable language and asks the parent to remove clothing (the diaper may stay on). For **head circumference**, the infant lies on the back; the **flexible tape** goes under the head **about an inch above the ears** and is pulled **snugly around the forehead**, with the parent helping if needed; the measurement is read to the nearest **¼, ½ or ¾ inch**, **repeated** and the two readings **averaged**. For **chest circumference**, the tape is placed under an infant at the **nipple line** while lying down (a 2- or 3-year-old may sit or stand), joined at the zero mark **midsternal at nipple level** and read to the nearest ¼ inch. The **head circumference is documented on the growth chart** and the **chest circumference in the chart**; hand hygiene and communication complete the skill (33 points; 70% is 23.1).',
order('Measure head and chest circumference (HOSA Skill IV)',['Hand hygiene; greet, identify and explain','Ask the parent to remove clothing except the diaper','Lay the infant on the back','Place the tape about an inch above the ears and snug around the forehead','Read to the nearest ¼ inch; repeat and average','Place the tape at the nipple line for the chest','Join at the zero mark midsternal and read','Plot head circumference on the growth chart; chart chest circumference']),
{t:'example',title:'Average two readings',prompt:'Two head circumference readings are 16¼ inches and 16¾ inches. What is the final measurement?',steps:['Add the readings: 16.25 + 16.75 = 33.0 inches.','Divide by 2: 33.0 ÷ 2 = 16.5 inches.'],answer:'16½ inches, plotted on the growth chart. (Values are invented for practice.)'}]],
['Well-child care and immunizations',[
'Medical assistants support **well-child visits** by measuring growth, screening development, vision and hearing, and preparing **immunizations** according to the **CDC immunization schedule**. Before vaccines, they provide the **Vaccine Information Statement (VIS)**, screen for contraindications and document the vaccine, lot number, manufacturer, site, route, date and the VIS edition given. Communicating with children means explaining in simple terms, allowing the parent to hold the child and being honest about discomfort. Safety includes never leaving an infant unattended on the exam table and keeping one hand on the child.',
kase('A growth concern',{a:{text:'A 9-month-old’s head circumference has moved from the 50th to the 95th percentile since the last visit. What should you do?',choices:[['Re-measure carefully, then report the change to the provider','b','Correct. Confirm accuracy and report significant changes in trend.',true],['Ignore it because percentiles do not matter','b','Crossing percentile lines may need evaluation.',false]]},b:{text:'You re-measure and get the same result. How do you document it?',choices:[['Plot the measurement on the growth chart and note the change for the provider','c','Correct. Accurate plotting shows the trend.',true],['Average it with last visit’s measurement','c','Each visit’s value is plotted separately.',false]]},c:{text:'The provider examines the infant and orders follow-up based on the documented trend.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['12-lead ECG: 10 electrodes; V1/V2 fourth intercostal space at the sternal borders, V4 fifth space midclavicular, V5 anterior axillary, V6 midaxillary; recognize artifacts.','Snellen at 20 feet (20/40 meaning), Jaeger at 14–16 inches; Skill VI: right eye first with the left covered, record the smallest line with one or fewer mistakes and note squinting.','Skill IV: head tape about an inch above the ears and around the forehead, read to the nearest ¼ inch, repeat and average; chest at the nipple line; plot head circumference on the growth chart.'],terms:[['Spirometry','Test of how much and how fast air is exhaled.'],['Snellen chart','Distance vision chart read at 20 feet.'],['Growth chart','Graph comparing a child’s measurements with percentiles.']],mcqs:[q('A standard 12-lead ECG uses how many electrodes?','10','12','6','4','Four limb and six chest electrodes.'),q('V4 is placed at the:','Fifth intercostal space, left midclavicular line','Fourth intercostal space, right sternal border','Left midaxillary line','Right arm','V1 and V2 are at the fourth space.'),q('A Snellen result of 20/40 means the patient:','Reads at 20 feet what a normal eye reads at 40 feet','Has normal vision','Reads at 40 feet','Is legally blind','Larger denominators mean poorer acuity.'),q('In Skill VI, which eye is tested first?','The right eye, with the left covered','The left eye, with the right covered','Both are never tested together','It does not matter','Then the left eye.'),q('The Jaeger card is held about:','14–16 inches from the eyes','20 feet away','2 inches away','6 feet away','It tests near vision.'),q('In Skill IV, head circumference is measured:','About an inch above the ears and around the forehead','Around the neck','At the nipple line','Around the chin','Read to the nearest ¼ inch.'),q('In Skill IV, after repeating the head measurement, the competitor:','Averages the two readings','Uses the larger reading','Uses the smaller reading','Discards both','The average is recorded.'),q('Spirometry measures:','Lung volumes and airflow such as FVC and FEV₁','Heart rhythm','Hearing','Visual acuity','Coach maximal effort.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would screen a patient’s distance and near vision following HOSA Skill VI, and how you would measure an infant’s head and chest circumference following Skill IV.',rubric:[['Vision setup',['glasses','contact','20 feet','occluder','both eyes open']],['Vision recording',['right eye','left eye','one or fewer','smallest line','squint','jaeger','14']],['Head circumference',['above the ears','forehead','snug','¼','repeat','average']],['Chest and documentation',['nipple line','zero','midsternal','growth chart','chart','hand hygiene']]],model:'For vision, I would perform hand hygiene, greet and identify the patient, note whether they wear glasses or contacts and explain that they will read the lines I point to while keeping both eyes open and covering one eye. Standing 20 feet from the Snellen chart, the patient reads with both eyes, then covers the left eye with the occluder and reads with the right, and I record the smallest line read with one or fewer mistakes, such as 20/30; then I repeat with the right eye covered. I would note squinting or head turning. For near vision, the patient sits up straight in good light, holds the Jaeger card 14–16 inches away and reads aloud with both eyes, first with and then without corrective lenses, and I record results, thank the patient, clean the occluder and perform hand hygiene. For the infant, after hand hygiene, identification and explanation, I would have the parent undress the baby except the diaper and lay the infant on the back. I would place the flexible tape about an inch above the ears and snugly around the forehead, read to the nearest quarter inch, repeat and average the readings, then measure the chest at the nipple line, joining the tape at zero midsternally. I would plot the head circumference on the growth chart, record the chest circumference in the chart and perform hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_ECG,MEDLINE_VA,CDC_GROWTH,CDC_IMZ]};
